From: chrshdl <cwasilei@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 07:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [GStreamer][WebAudio] Clock the pipeline by the audio sink

The pipeline picks its clock on going to PLAYING, when the sink of the
live webaudiosrc has no ring buffer yet to provide its clock with, so
it ran on the system clock, and the sink was slaved to it. An audio
device's clock drifts from the system clock (a USB speakerphone here
plays about 110-170 ppm slow), and the sink corrects that by jumping
tens of milliseconds once the drift is large enough, every few minutes.
With the source held to the system clock by the clocksync, it could not
render ahead to fill the sink again: after such a jump every 10 ms
segment played in part and in part as silence, as noise that faded out
only as fast as the clocks drifted, for many seconds.

Clock the pipeline by the sink instead, and let the device pace the
rendering, without the clocksync: silent quanta are rendered as zeros,
rather than flagged as gaps that the sink drops, so they fill the sink
like any audio and the source blocks while it is full. That also keeps
the source from running ahead on gaps, which the clocksync was added
for (https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=296843). And it keeps the
sink from starting over after each silence: with gaps, the first 100 ms
or so of each sound after one were lost, so every tone a page played
after a pause played cut.

Starting, the sink and the appsrc's queue took the first quanta as fast
as they rendered, until full, and the context's time jumped ahead by
about 120 ms, past what a page schedules right after resuming the
context, such as a tone at its currentTime plus 10 ms, which then played
cut. So the source first fills them with silence it does not render, as
much as the sink's buffer-time and latency-time, which the destination
now both sets, and the appsrc's queue hold.

The buffers' timestamps carried on across stops, such as a suspend of the
context, while each start begins a segment whose running time starts at
0: after a resume, the clocksync held the first buffer back for as long
as the source had played before, which grew with each suspend (resume()
took 1.5, 3, 4.4, 5.8 s), and with the sink clocking the pipeline it
played that long as silence. So the timestamps start over with each
start, and the output's position, which the context reports, counts the
rendered samples on across starts, as before.

The destination did not report its output latency, so the context's
outputLatency was 0, and a page could not tell when what it rendered is
heard, such as to open a microphone only after a tone has played. It is
now how far the source has pushed ahead of the position the sink plays
at, by the device's clock: with a USB speakerphone on a Raspberry Pi 4,
218-222 ms, within 1 ms of the latency measured at the device. Right
after starting it is less, until the device plays: the sink's position
is 0 until then.

Upstream: not submitted yet

Signed-off-by: chrshdl <cwasilei@gmail.com>
